from_file "fortigate.log" {
  read_lines
}
// FortiGate emits many differently shaped records, and `parse_kv` gives each
// field set its own schema. Tenzir splits a batch whenever the schema changes,
// so an interleaved stream degenerates into single-event batches and the
// mapping below runs per event. `unordered` tells the engine that only
// intra-schema order matters, letting it demultiplex the stream into
// homogeneous batches. The mapping is stateless per event, so nothing depends
// on the order between events. Measured at ~10x throughput on mixed FortiGate
// logs; see docs/fortigate-ocsf.md.
unordered {
  fortinet = line.parse_kv()
}
fortinet::fortigate::ocsf::map event=fortinet
fortinet.raw_data = move line
fortinet.raw_data_size = fortinet.raw_data.length_bytes()
this = fortinet
ocsf::derive
